  <h2 id="h264">The Industry Standard: H.264 (AVC)</h2>
  <p>H.264, also known as Advanced Video Coding (AVC), is the most widely used video codec in the world. Introduced in 2003, it powers Blu-ray discs, YouTube streams, and almost every video you capture on a smartphone.</p>
  <p>Its main advantage today is <strong>100% universal compatibility</strong>. Any device manufactured in the last 15 years has dedicated hardware to decode H.264 efficiently.</p>

  <h2 id="h265">The Successor: H.265 (HEVC)</h2>
  <p>H.265, or High Efficiency Video Coding (HEVC), was designed to replace H.264, especially for 4K and 8K content. It is significantly more efficient at compressing video data.</p>
  <p>H.265 can deliver the exact same visual quality as H.264 but at <strong>roughly half the file size</strong>. Alternatively, it can deliver vastly better quality at the same file size.</p>

